theory_blocks.body:
Neither control method in this lesson produces a completely pollution-free result. Flue-gas desulfurisation reduces the sulfur dioxide released, but some may still remain depending on how efficient the process is;

it does not remove every other pollutant in the flue gas. A catalytic converter converts carbon monoxide and nitrogen monoxide specifically;

it does not touch the carbon dioxide already present in the exhaust, and does not prevent a vehicle from emitting carbon dioxide at all, since carbon dioxide is an unavoidable product of burning a carbon-containing fuel.

[[red:'Reduces emissions of a named pollutant']] and [[green:'makes emissions harmless overall']] are two different claims, and only the first is supported by either method.

exercises.full_solution:
(a) Natural sources of methane besides cattle, rice fields and landfill:

- **wetlands and marshes**, where methane-producing bacteria break down plant matter in waterlogged, low-oxygen soil, the same conditions as a flooded rice field;
- **thawing permafrost**, releasing methane trapped in frozen ground for a very long time;
- **termites**, which host methane-producing microorganisms in their gut, as cattle do;
- **the oceans**, from seabed sediments and methane hydrates;
- natural seepage from underground gas deposits, and the burning of vegetation in **wildfires**.

Thawing permafrost is worth knowing because it is a feedback loop: warming releases methane, and methane causes further warming.

(b) The answer depends on where you live, so research your own region. Possible local effects include more frequent or more intense heatwaves, wetter or drier seasons, more flooding or more wildfire risk, rising sea level on low-lying coasts, and changes in which crops, plants and animals can thrive locally.
